; 12-def.kurt

; `def` introduces a brand-new constant together with its defining equation
; (`=`) or equivalence (`iff`).  Unlike `use`, which just asserts a formula,
; `def` also checks that exactly one *new* symbol is being introduced, and
; only on the left-hand side.

; Neither `=` nor `iff` exist in the bare core language -- they come from
; theories, so we `load` first (lesson 10):
load propositional
load equality

def p iff true         ; introduces `p`, defined to mean the same as `true`
def q iff p             ; `q` is defined in terms of the previously defined `p`
def x = 18               ; `=` works too, e.g. for naming a particular value

x = 18
p implies q              ; definitions unfold directly, in both directions
use p
q

; A definition can also have arguments: the variable `$x` on the left-hand side stands for
; any argument, so this defines a function `f` (`+` comes from `number.kurt`):
load number
def f($x) = $x + $x
f(3) = 3 + 3

; A `def` may also stand inside a proof (or any other block, lessons 18-21): there it is a name
; for this proof only. It is gone when the block closes, and the result of the block must not
; mention it -- so a later proof may define `d` anew.
load logic
show ∃ z (z = 3 + 3)
proof
    def d = 3 + 3            ; a name for the witness, in this proof only
    d = 3 + 3
    ∃ z (z = 3 + 3)          ; `d` is the witness
qed
